The browser agent lacked the MCP browser tool (plugin's MCP server not connected — the superpowers-chrome MCP server isn't registered in this project's .mcp.json; only prax is). So no MCP route.

Remaining options for REAL browser evidence:
1. Node + puppeteer-core + local Edge/Chrome — needs shell access. My session has no Bash. But agent "general-purpose" has Tools: * — which should include Bash (built-in). The subagent environment may have a fuller toolset like the browser agent had Read/Grep/Glob/Skill only... The browser agent's restriction came from its own definition (read-only browser analysis). general-purpose declares "All tools: *" — likely includes Bash, Write, etc.

2. codex agents: codex:codex-rescue has Bash too.

Try general-purpose agent with instructions to write a puppeteer-core script and run it. Concern: general-purpose might also lack Bash if the whole harness lacks it... but the harness statement "Tools: *" strongly suggests Bash exists for agents. Also the system prompt says "Prefer the dedicated file/search tools over shell commands" implying shell exists in agent-land.

Plan for the general-purpose agent (foreground):
- Check node: `node -v`.
- `npm init -y` + `npm i puppeteer-core` in a tools subfolder (keep deliverable clean: use `evidence/` for outputs and `tools/` for script; node_modules in tools/).
- Find Edge/Chrome executable (standard paths; puppeteer-core channel: 'edge'? puppeteer-core launch({executablePath})).
- Write shot.js runbook (I'll provide the exact script inline in the prompt to minimize agent creativity). Actually I can Write the script myself with the Write tool! Then the agent only needs to run `npm i puppeteer-core` and `node tools\shot.js`. Reduces agent freedom.

Even better: I write package.json + tools/shot.js; agent runs two commands and reports output. If node missing → report; fallback: codex agent.

shot.js details (puppeteer-core):
